SS reduction indicates the degradation of solid during the disintegration process. (Fig. 2a

                           ) depicts the variation of SS concentration during disintegration process. The samples used for disintegration are controlled (raw sludge without any treatment), flocculated sludge (bacterial disintegration only) and deflocculated sludge (TCTF mediated enzyme secreting bacterial disintegration). (Fig. 2a
                           ) elucidated that a hasty reduction of SS concentration from 4000 to 3914.58 mg/L, 3698.66 mg/L and 3500 mg/L in control, flocculated and deflocculated sludge due to the increment in disintegration time (from 0 to 42 h). The decrement in SS concentration promotes increment in SS reduction efficiency from 0 to 2.13%, 7.53% and 12.5%, respectively for control, flocculated and deflocculated sludge. This increment attributable to prompt intensification of hydrolytic activity causing solid biodegradability due to the release of enzymes from the inoculated bacterial strain. This pattern achieved in enzyme secreting bacterial disintegration for all samples was comparable but the solubilisation efficiency varies which was in accordance to the work of [26]. The relative analysis of flocculated and deflocculated sludge depicts that floc-disrupted sludge showed 5% higher SS reduction than flocculated sludge. Further, time increment causes the stabilization of SS reduction for all samples. The reason behind the stabilization is saturation of solubilisation by the inoculated bacteria because of complete substrate consumption. Hence, the disintegration time of 42 h is optimal for enzyme secreting bacterial disintegration. A significant difference in SS reduction between flocculated and deflocculated samples was analysed through a standard T test. The obtained p value was 0.0002 that was lesser than 0.05.

(Fig. 2b

                           ) explains the release of soluble organics and solubilisation efficiency with respect to disintegration time. Organics solubilisation is the process of converting or disintegrating particulate organics into pliable biodegradable compounds for cell lysis induced by microbial stress within the sludge [27]. Microbial stress headed to lysis and induced the intracellular substances to leak out of the microbial cell that promotes solubilisation. The parameters SCOD and COD solubilisation efficiency quantified the soluble organic release in the sludge. (Fig. 2b
                           ) detailed the SCOD release and solubilisation efficiency of control flocculated and deflocculated sludge. The profile of solubilisation obeyed the Gaussian plot with dual phases: liquefaction phase and depletion phase. Kavitha et al., [6], Sharmila et al., [7], Banu et al., [1] showed similar profile of solubilisation in the research work. In the liquefaction phase, the SCOD concentration increased from 0–123 mg/L, 0–441 mg/L and 0–808 mg/L, respectively for control, flocculated and deflocculated sludge, respectively up to the disintegration time of 42 h. This increment signifies that the liberated enzymes of the grown inoculated bacteria breaks the microbial cell to release of intracellular substances and liquefies them to the aqueous phase in the sludge [25]. The augmentation of disintegration time beyond 42 h promoted solubilisation decrement indicating depletion phase and resulted in null solubilisation at the end. This depletion phase implies the complete consumption of substrate in the sludge that promotes the utilization of solubilized organics by the inoculated bacteria [28]. The higher solubilisation of 1.69%, 8.2% and 16%, respectively were achieved for control, flocculated and deflocculated sludge. Of these three samples, the deflocculated sludge yielded better solubilization efficiency due to deflocculation that enabled quick accessibility of substrate by inoculated bacteria for the disintegration process. Control sludge showed scanty solubilization owing to the absence of inoculated bacterial strain to induce disintegration. Finally, the observed result clearly indicated that the solubilisation efficiency peaked at 42 h of disintegration time, which ought to be an optimum condition to promote better sludge disintegration. The standard T test correlation between the soluble organic release of flocculated and deflocculated sludge showed significant difference since the p value calculated was 0.006 which was lesser than 0.05.

(Fig. 4

                           ) elucidates the specific growth rate of inoculated bacterial cell with respect to disintegration time. It clearly described that the enzyme activity and specific growth rate falls into four distinct phases namely: Acclimatization phase (0–15 h), exponential phase (18–42 h), steady phase (48–72 h) and drop phase (78–92 h). A similar pattern of phase distinction was found in the literature of Ushani et al., [30]. In addition, it was evident that the intracellular enzyme (protease and amylase) tempted the growth of bacterial cell that determines the specific growth rate (μ). The minimal specific growth rate was observed in the acclimatization phase that is about 0.034–0.035 h−1. This slow growth rate indicates the acclimatization of the substrate to the sludge conditions and less potency of the enzyme to perform disintegration. During the exponential phase, the protease and amylase enzyme activity increases with the subsequent increment in growth rate. This increment infers the release of intracellular organics inducing solubilization. The maximum growth rate achieved at 42 h was 0.2 h−1 and enzymatic action (protease – 0.28 U/mL and amylase – 0.2 U/mL). In steady phase, there found a stability in μ and enzymatic action because of the saturation of hydrolytic activity. At last, μ and enzymatic action reaches drop phase. In drop phase, the inoculated bacteria lack efficiency in the growth rate and enzyme secretion attributable to the substrate depletion. In addition, (Fig. 4
                           ) details the soluble organic matter release (SOM). Up to the 42 h of disintegration time, the SOM increases and reached a maximum of 440 mg/L owing to increased enzyme activity and growth rate. Beyond 42 h, SOM decreases due to the reduced enzyme activity and growth rate. In accordance with the above discussion, disintegration time 42 h was an optimal duration for effective enzyme secreting bacteria disintegration.

Methane production from the control, flocculated and deflocculated sludge was used to investigate the efficiency of TCTF mediated enzyme secreting bacterial disintegration. The methane production of control, flocculated and deflocculated sludge was observed in (Fig. 5a

                        ) Due to the acclimatization of the inoculum to the substrate environment, scanty methane production was seen up to 5 days for all samples (Control – 13.57 mL/g COD, flocculated – 48.51 mL/g COD, deflocculated – 83.62 mL/g COD). Beyond 5 days, the methane generation increases gradually and reached a maximum methane production rate of 0.978 mL/g COD.d (control), 11.14 mL/g COD.d (flocculated) and 21.03 mL/g COD.d (deflocculated) at 15 days. The maximum achieved methane generation was 20.405 mL/g COD, 81.45 mL/g COD and 140.40 mL/g COD, respectively for control, flocculated and deflocculated sludge samples. This increment implies higher methanogens inhabitants that is due to better consumption of substrates by the methanogens. Also, it was evident that the deflocculated sludge showed higher methane generation than control and flocculated sludge. This confirms that deflocculation and disintegration process enhances the easy accessibility of substrate by the methanogens promoting higher methane yield. However, poor substrate accessibility of substrate in control hinders the methanogenic activity promoting very less methane production. Complete consumption of substrate beyond 15 days resulted in inadequate and stabilized methane production. This in turn, starts the endogenous respiration beyond 15 days which leads to the decrement in methane production rate. The substrate biodegradability and hydrolysis rate of all three sludge samples was estimated via non-linear regression. The obtained parameters: hydrolysis rate constant and biodegradability were given in (Fig. 5b

                        ). Each ellipse indicates 95% confidence for control, flocculated and deflocculated sludge samples. The effect of deflocculation and disintegration was included in the confidence region which was increased gradually. The biodegradability and hydrolysis constant during proposed treatment was also estimated. The higher biodegradability and hydrolysis constant was achieved in deflocculated sludge (Xd = 0.185 gCOD/gCOD; ɵ = 0.191 d−1) than flocculated (Xd = 0.102 gCOD/gCOD; ɵ = 0.16 d−1) and control (Xd = 0.048 gCOD/gCOD; ɵ = 0.108 d−1) sludge.

This analysis of the current research was performed to detail the possibility of treatment process in pilot scale [31]. Based on the research of Eswari et al., [32], the treatment efficiency of the proposed method was investigated using the attained investigational outcomes for one ton of sludge (assumed). Energy intake and profit of treatment process was evaluated preceding to cost estimation. Detailed energetic, solid and cost analysis was carried out to assess the proficiency of the TCTF mediated enzyme secreting bacterial pretreatment that was provided in (Fig. 6

                        ). Energetic balance deliberated the energy depleted and gained during the treatment process. The energy depleted in the form of stirring, pumping and heat loss. The total energy consumed during treatment for flocculated and deflocculated sludge was estimated to be −441.48, and −442 kW h, respectively. Gained energy was the methane achieved in flocculated and deflocculated sludge as 225, and 468.44 kWh, respectively. Taking the difference of depleted and gained energy gives the net energy. From the observed (Fig. 6
                        ), the deflocculated samples documented high net energy of 26.44 kWh which signifies the efficiency of TCTF mediated enzyme secreting bacterial disintegration. Less net energy achieved in flocculated (−216.48 kWh) sludge.
